# Platform/SRE triage task set for Session 15 Part 2.
# THIS FILE IS DATA — replace it wholesale.
#
# One JSON object per line: {"id", "task", "expectation", "difficulty"}.
# Every task is self-contained: no live cluster, no Jenkins, no Jira. The
# success criterion is a checkable operational fact, not a style preference.
#
#   python proofs/p1_cost_per_task.py --tasks proofs/tasks/sre_triage.jsonl --principal course/s15/p1
{"id": "s01_api_version_pod", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "What Kubernetes API version do you pass when listing Pods? Reply with the version string only.", "expectation": "States v1, and does not name apps/v1 or batch/v1 as the Pod API version."}
{"id": "s02_api_version_deploy", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "What Kubernetes API version do you pass when listing Deployments? Reply with the version string only.", "expectation": "States apps/v1."}
{"id": "s03_http_402", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "An LLM gateway refuses a call because the remaining spend budget cannot cover the projected cost. What HTTP status code should it return? Reply with the number only.", "expectation": "States 402."}
{"id": "s04_kubectl_events", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Name the kubectl subcommand that lists Kubernetes events. Reply with the two-word command only, no flags.", "expectation": "States kubectl get events, or equivalently get events."}
{"id": "s05_imagepull_first", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A Pod is stuck in ImagePullBackOff. List the first three places you inspect, in order, as a numbered list of one short phrase each. Do not invent cluster-specific names.", "expectation": "Starts with events (or describe) for the pull error, then checks the image tag/registry, then node or service-account IAM / pull-secret credentials. Must not start with application logs as the first step."}
{"id": "s06_crashloop_first", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. Give the first three kubectl commands you would run, in order, each on its own line. Use placeholder names my-ns and my-pod.", "expectation": "Includes kubectl logs (current), kubectl logs with --previous (or equivalent previous-container logs), and either kubectl describe pod or kubectl get events. Order must put logs before speculative restarts or image rebuilds."}
{"id": "s07_pending_first", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A Pod stays Pending. Name the single most informative first command, using placeholders my-ns and my-pod, and in one sentence say what you are looking for in its output.", "expectation": "Uses kubectl describe pod (or get events) and looks for scheduling failure: insufficient CPU/memory, node selectors, taints/tolerations, or PVC unbound. Must not treat application logs as the first step for a Pending pod."}
{"id": "s08_circuit_cooldown", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A gateway circuit breaker records a provider ReadTimeout whose str() is empty, then looks up the cooldown using only the exception message. What cooldown does it apply, and what should it inspect instead so a timeout is recognised? Answer in two sentences.", "expectation": "States that matching on an empty message yields a zero-second cooldown (the provider is immediately eligible again), and that the fix is to inspect the exception class name as well as the message, for example ReadTimeout."}
{"id": "s09_jql_open_hcat", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Write one Jira JQL query that returns issues in project HCAT whose status is Open. Reply with the JQL only.", "expectation": "Contains project = HCAT (or project = \"HCAT\") and status = Open. No extra filters that would hide Open issues."}
{"id": "s10_idp_nesting", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "An IDP stack spec wrongly nests the entire spec, including s3_map, inside one secrets_map entry's object_instance. In two or three sentences: what will happen on apply, and what is the correct shape for object_instance?", "expectation": "Warns that nesting other *_map sections inside object_instance can delete or fail to create unrelated resources. Correct shape: object_instance holds only that resource's own fields; s3_map and secrets_map stay as sibling top-level sections."}
{"id": "s11_eks_api_table", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Fill this table. For each Kind give the API version used when listing it: Pod, Deployment, Job, Ingress, HorizontalPodAutoscaler. Return five lines of the form Kind: version.", "expectation": "Pod: v1; Deployment: apps/v1; Job: batch/v1; Ingress: networking.k8s.io/v1; HorizontalPodAutoscaler: autoscaling/v2. Any of those five wrong fails."}
{"id": "s12_jenkins_fail", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A Jenkins pipeline job CloudOps/Orchestrator/master-pipeline failed. List the four inspection steps you take, in order, naming the operation in each step (not a vendor-specific function name). One short phrase per step.", "expectation": "Order starts from the build result, then the build log (or error search in the log), then SCM revision, then change sets. Must not start with retriggering the pipeline as the first diagnostic step."}
{"id": "s13_budget_projection", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A budget controller projects cost as estimated_input_tokens * input_price + max_tokens * output_price. A call has estimated_input_tokens=800, input_price=$0.30 per million, max_tokens=1024, output_price=$1.20 per million. Remaining budget is $0.00080. Should the call be admitted? Show the projected cost and the decision.", "expectation": "Computes projected cost as 800*0.30/1e6 + 1024*1.20/1e6 = $0.0014688 (accept nearby rounding). Remaining $0.00080 is less than that, so the call is refused, not admitted."}
{"id": "s14_break_even", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A cheap model costs c per call and a frontier model costs C per resolved task, with measured price ratio C/c = 9.2. A successful cheap task uses 1 call; a failed cheap task consumes k=3 calls (first attempt plus two retries). Below what resolution rate r* does the cheap model become more expensive per resolved task than the frontier? Give r* as a percentage to one decimal place and the formula you used.", "expectation": "Uses r* = k / (C/c - 1 + k) = 3 / (9.2 - 1 + 3) = 3/11.2 ≈ 26.8%. An inverted formula, or reporting the complementary failure rate 73.2% as if it were r*, fails."}
{"id": "s15_gemini_pool", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Routing config lists the logical provider name gemini first, but the process registered gemini_1 .. gemini_5. Membership is tested with the literal \"gemini\" in providers. In three sentences: what happens at routing time, and what is the correct expansion?", "expectation": "Explains that \"gemini\" is not a registered key so Gemini is silently skipped despite being first in the ring. The fix is to expand the base name gemini to all live gemini_N instances."}
{"id": "s16_empty_bill", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A provider returns HTTP 200, out_tokens=512, stop_reason=max_tokens, visible content empty, and a non-zero cost. In four sentences: where did the tokens go, why is HTTP 200 the wrong success signal, and what four fields must be metered together?", "expectation": "Identifies hidden reasoning consuming the output ceiling; HTTP 200 means the transport succeeded, not that useful work happened; metering must include tokens charged, visible output, stop reason, and task result."}
{"id": "s17_semantic_vs_prompt", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Contrast prompt caching and semantic caching in a four-row comparison covering: what is reused, whether the model still runs, the main saving, and the main risk. Then state the cosine threshold this session uses and why the cache is opt-in.", "expectation": "Prompt cache reuses a byte-exact input prefix and still runs the model, saving discounted input; risk is a cache miss. Semantic cache reuses a previous answer, skips the model, saves the whole call; risk is a wrong-answer collision. Threshold 0.95, opt-in because a false match returns an old answer with no model chance to correct it."}
{"id": "s18_yaml_nesting_fix", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Rewrite this malformed IDP fragment so each resource is a top-level map entry and object_instance holds only that resource's fields. Return YAML only.\n\nsecrets_map:\n- name: app-secret\n  object_instance:\n    secret_name: app-secret\n    s3_map:\n    - name: app-bucket\n      object_instance:\n        s3_name: app-bucket\n", "expectation": "Produces two sibling sections, secrets_map and s3_map. The secret's object_instance has secret_name (and not s3_map). The bucket's object_instance has s3_name. No resource map nested inside another object's object_instance."}
